1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:07,468
Welcome to Episode Two. This is an extended lesson under subtopic seven point one, the Power of Three.

2
00:00:07,888 --> 00:00:14,992
We will work through a complete bullish case and a complete bearish case, then study five ways the model fails.

3
00:00:15,412 --> 00:00:20,116
Finally, we connect PO3 with what you learned in Levels Two to Six.

4
00:00:21,936 --> 00:00:28,416
Here is the bullish case in full. We begin with the daily open at four thousand two hundred and sixty-eight.

5
00:00:28,836 --> 00:00:34,980
Price builds an accumulation range around the open. We mark the range high and the range low.

6
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:41,952
Two touches of the low leave equal lows. Sell-side liquidity is assumed to rest beneath them.

7
00:00:42,372 --> 00:00:50,556
Then price drops, below the open and through the range low. The sweep is visible: the wick trades clearly beneath the level.

8
00:00:50,976 --> 00:00:56,424
Next comes the reclaim. A strong bullish candle closes back above the range low.

9
00:00:56,844 --> 00:01:01,284
Displacement follows, and the move leaves a bullish fair value gap.

10
00:01:01,704 --> 00:01:09,552
Price closes above the last lower high, a market structure shift, and then above the range high, a break of structure.

11
00:01:09,972 --> 00:01:13,476
Now the distribution phase. Price expands upward.

12
00:01:13,896 --> 00:01:18,744
It runs toward buy-side liquidity above the old high, and trades through it.

13
00:01:19,164 --> 00:01:26,220
Every step is in order and each one is visible on the chart. That is what makes this a complete illustration.

14
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:36,488
What separates a valid illustration from an incomplete formation? First, a clear reference, and identifiable range boundaries.

15
00:01:36,908 --> 00:01:43,364
Second, a sweep that genuinely trades through the level, followed by a reclaim and displacement.

16
00:01:43,784 --> 00:01:49,328
Third, a structure shift in the new direction, and expansion away from the range.

17
00:01:49,748 --> 00:01:58,292
An incomplete formation is missing one or more of these. Perhaps there is no real range, or the so-called sweep never crossed the level.

18
00:01:58,712 --> 00:02:04,040
Perhaps price never reclaims, or no displacement or structure shift appears.

19
00:02:04,460 --> 00:02:10,124
And be honest about hindsight. Labels drawn only after the move are not analysis.

20
00:02:11,944 --> 00:02:16,264
Now the bearish case. Same daily open, opposite sequence.

21
00:02:16,684 --> 00:02:19,612
An accumulation range forms around the open.

22
00:02:20,032 --> 00:02:25,288
Equal highs at the top of the range suggest buy-side liquidity resting above.

23
00:02:25,708 --> 00:02:31,612
Price rallies above the open and trades through the range high. That is the possible buy-side sweep.

24
00:02:32,032 --> 00:02:36,664
Price cannot hold. It is rejected and returns below the range high.

25
00:02:37,084 --> 00:02:41,380
Bearish displacement follows, leaving a bearish fair value gap.

26
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:49,744
A close below the last higher low gives a market structure shift, and a close below the range low confirms a break of structure.

27
00:02:50,164 --> 00:02:55,228
Distribution runs lower, toward sell-side liquidity beneath the old low.

28
00:02:55,648 --> 00:02:59,584
Again, every element is visible, and in order.

29
00:03:01,404 --> 00:03:06,036
Failure one. Price sweeps the range low, but it never reclaims.

30
00:03:06,456 --> 00:03:13,704
It keeps closing below the level and falls further. The bullish reading was simply wrong. The sweep was a breakdown.

31
00:03:14,124 --> 00:03:19,572
Failure two. Price breaks out of the range and continues in the breakout direction.

32
00:03:19,992 --> 00:03:27,432
There is no false move at all. Someone waiting for manipulation first would miss the move, or worse, trade against it.

33
00:03:27,852 --> 00:03:36,108
In both cases, the sweep or breakout was only a possible manipulation until reversal evidence appeared, and it never did.

34
00:03:37,928 --> 00:03:44,048
Failure three. The sweep happens and price recovers a little, but it simply goes back to ranging.

35
00:03:44,468 --> 00:03:49,748
There is no meaningful distribution phase. No expansion, no new structure.

36
00:03:50,168 --> 00:03:55,832
Failure four. Price expands briefly, then falls back into the original range.

37
00:03:56,252 --> 00:04:00,908
The early part looked like PO3, but the expansion did not hold.

38
00:04:01,328 --> 00:04:09,152
In both cases, the sequence is incomplete. Manage the idea as failed, rather than stretching the labels to fit.

39
00:04:10,972 --> 00:04:14,908
Failure five: price forms a different structure altogether.

40
00:04:15,328 --> 00:04:19,408
Here it sweeps the range low, then sweeps the range high as well.

41
00:04:19,828 --> 00:04:22,828
Neither move is followed by a sustained expansion.

42
00:04:23,248 --> 00:04:28,120
This is a widening or rotating range, not a Power of Three sequence.

43
00:04:28,540 --> 00:04:36,316
The professional response is to stand aside and re-read the structure, instead of forcing a PO3 label onto it.

44
00:04:38,136 --> 00:04:45,864
Now let’s connect PO3 with earlier levels. Level Two, market structure, confirms the shift after the sweep.

45
00:04:46,284 --> 00:04:52,164
Level Three, liquidity, tells us where the sweep happened and where the objective may be.

46
00:04:52,584 --> 00:04:58,920
Level Four adds institutional price zones, such as an order block near the manipulation extreme.

47
00:04:59,340 --> 00:05:05,580
Level Five adds displacement and fair value gaps as evidence of a genuine reversal.

48
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:13,080
Level Six asks whether manipulation happened in discount for a bullish model, or premium for a bearish one.

49
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:19,692
These factors support an interpretation. They are not mandatory conditions for every PO3.

50
00:05:21,512 --> 00:05:26,912
Here is the bullish case with each lens added. Level Two: the market structure shift.

51
00:05:27,332 --> 00:05:31,868
Level Three: the sell-side sweep, and the buy-side objective above.

52
00:05:32,288 --> 00:05:39,896
Level Four: the last down-close candle before displacement, which Level Four defines as a bullish order block.

53
00:05:40,316 --> 00:05:44,084
Level Five: the displacement and its fair value gap.

54
00:05:44,504 --> 00:05:53,624
Level Six: measured from the sweep low to the old high, the accumulation and the sweep both sat in discount, below equilibrium.

55
00:05:54,044 --> 00:05:57,908
Each lens adds context. None of them guarantees the move.

56
00:05:59,728 --> 00:06:07,576
To summarise. A complete PO3 shows every step in order, and the sweep must visibly trade through the level.

57
00:06:07,996 --> 00:06:12,292
Reclaim, displacement and a structure shift are your evidence.

58
00:06:12,712 --> 00:06:21,784
We saw five failure types: no reversal, a clean breakout, no distribution, a return to the range, and a different structure.

59
00:06:22,204 --> 00:06:29,308
Earlier levels add context, not mandatory rules. And if the sequence is incomplete, do not force it.

60
00:06:31,128 --> 00:06:36,864
Time to check your understanding. Pause the video and answer these five questions in your own words.

61
00:06:38,784 --> 00:06:44,208
Write down your reasoning, not just a one-word answer. The reasoning is what we assess.

62
00:06:48,028 --> 00:06:54,244
Here are the short answers. Compare them with your reasoning; the workbook explains each one in full.

63
